<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 3 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered 1-8 in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Add more by appending another <exercise> below; numbering of the
     existing ones will not shift. -->

<exercises xml:id="ch3-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-nash-definition">
    <title>The Nash Equilibrium Definition</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose we have a game with <m>N</m> players (<m>N \geq 3</m>), and
        let <m>s^* = (s_1^*, s_2^*, \ldots, s_N^*)</m> be a strategy profile.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-nash-definition-math">
      <statement>
        <p>
          State, mathematically, what it means for <m>s^*</m> to be a Nash
          equilibrium. Your statement must be written in terms of the
          payoff functions <m>\pi_i</m>, using notation for an
          <m>N</m>-player game.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-nash-definition-not">
      <statement>
        <p>
          State, mathematically, what it means for <m>s^*</m> to
          <em>not</em> be a Nash equilibrium, again in terms of the payoff
          functions <m>\pi_i</m>. Then explain in words what your
          statement says.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-nash-definition-matrix">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the two player game below. Player 1 chooses a row from
          <m>S_1 = \{a, b, c\}</m> and player 2 chooses a column from
          <m>S_2 = \{x, y, z\}</m>, with payoffs listed in the order
          (player 1, player 2).
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table-hw-three-profiles">
          <title>A <m>3 \times 3</m> game</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
            <col halign="left" right="minor" />
            <col right="minor" />
            <col right="minor" />
            <col />
            <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
              <cell></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
              <cell>Player 2: <m>z</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
              <cell>(4, 3)</cell>
              <cell>(0, 1)</cell>
              <cell>(1, 2)</cell>
            </row>
            <row bottom="minor">
              <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
              <cell>(2, 0)</cell>
              <cell>(5, 5)</cell>
              <cell>(0, 6)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
              <cell>(1, 3)</cell>
              <cell>(6, 0)</cell>
              <cell>(3, 3)</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
          For each of the following strategy profiles, determine whether it
          is a Nash equilibrium, justifying your answer with payoff
          comparisons. If it is a Nash equilibrium, say whether it is
          strict.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><p><m>(a, x)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>(b, y)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>(c, z)</m></p></li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-water-pollution-five">
    <title>Water Pollution with Five Firms</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is a larger version of the Water Pollution game from
        <xref ref="read-3-3" text="title" />. Five firms share a lake.
        Each firm chooses to <term>purify</term> its wastewater, at a cost
        of 1, or to <term>pollute</term>, at no cost. If 3 or more firms
        pollute, the lake is fouled and <em>every</em> firm pays an
        additional cleanup cost of 3. A firm's payoff is the negative of
        its total cost. For example:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If only firm 1 pollutes, the lake stays clean. Firm 1 gets
            <m>0</m>, and firms 2 through 5 each get <m>-1</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If firms 1 through 4 pollute, the lake is fouled. Firms 1
            through 4 each get <m>-3</m>, and firm 5 gets
            <m>-1 - 3 = -4</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        There are <m>2^5 = 32</m> strategy profiles, but you should not
        check them one at a time. Since all firms are treated the same, the
        analysis of a profile depends only on how many firms pollute.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-water-pollution-five-k">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>k</m> be the number of polluting firms. For each
          <m>k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</m>, determine whether the profiles with
          exactly <m>k</m> polluters are Nash equilibria. Justify each
          answer.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-water-pollution-five-all">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe all Nash equilibria of the game, and determine how many
          there are.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash">
    <title>Locating Two Banks, Revisited</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Return to the two competing banks of
        <xref ref="hw-bank-location" />: each bank chooses a block
        <m>s_i \in \{1, 2, \ldots, 99\}</m> along a 99-block avenue, each
        block has 100 residents, residents use the closer bank, and a block
        equidistant from both banks splits its residents evenly. The payoff
        to each bank is its total number of customers. In that exercise we
        used iterated elimination to show that <m>(50, 50)</m> is a
        dominant strategy equilibrium.
      </p>

      <p>
        As before, please do not write out a <m>99 \times 99</m> matrix.
        Fix one bank's location and think about how the other bank's
        payoff changes as it moves.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash-center">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show directly, without using iterated elimination, that
          <m>(50, 50)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash-apart">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that any strategy profile <m>(p, q)</m> where
          <m>p \lt q</m> is not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Split into two cases: the banks are not adjacent
          (<m>q \geq p + 2</m>), and the banks are adjacent
          (<m>q = p + 1</m>).
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-bank-location-nash-same">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that any strategy profile <m>(p, q)</m> where
          <m>p = q \neq 50</m> is not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-resource-game">
    <title>Bidding the Resource</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Each of two players has 1 (divisible) unit of a resource. Player 1
        chooses some amount <m>s_1 \in [0, 1]</m>, and player 2
        simultaneously chooses some amount <m>s_2 \in [0, 1]</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The player that chooses the higher amount wins the total of what
        remains, <m>(1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2)</m>. The player that chooses the
        lower amount gets a payoff of zero, forfeiting the remaining amount
        to the other player. In the case of a tie, they split what remains.
        The payoffs are:
      </p>

      <me>
        \pi_1(s_1, s_2) =
        \begin{cases}
          (1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2), \amp s_1 \gt s_2 \\
          1 - s_1, \amp s_1 = s_2 \\
          0, \amp s_1 \lt s_2
        \end{cases}
      </me>
      <me>
        \pi_2(s_1, s_2) =
        \begin{cases}
          (1 - s_1) + (1 - s_2), \amp s_2 \gt s_1 \\
          1 - s_2, \amp s_1 = s_2 \\
          0, \amp s_2 \lt s_1
        \end{cases}
      </me>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-one-one">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>s_1 = s_2 = 1</m> is a Nash equilibrium. Is it a
          strict Nash equilibrium? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-below-one">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that there are no Nash equilibria where
          <m>s_2 \lt s_1 \lt 1</m>, by showing that player 2 can improve.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-at-one">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose <m>s_2 \lt s_1 = 1</m>. Explain why player 2's
          deviation from part (b) no longer works, and show that the
          profile is still not a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-resource-game-tie">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that there are no Nash equilibria where
          <m>s_1 = s_2 \lt 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> There are infinitely many strategy profiles
        in this game, and parts (a) through (d) break them into exactly
        four cases. The cases we did not explicitly consider are handled
        through symmetry. For example, part (b) also shows there are no
        Nash equilibria where <m>s_1 \lt s_2 \lt 1</m>. So <m>(1, 1)</m>
        is the only Nash equilibrium of this game.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-nash">
    <title>Hawk and Dove, Revisited</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Return to the Hawk-Dove game of <xref ref="hw-hawk-dove" />. Each
        player chooses <term>hawk</term> (<m>h</m>) or
        <term>dove</term> (<m>d</m>), where <m>v</m> is the value of the
        territory and <m>w</m> is the cost of getting injured.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-hawk-dove-nash">
        <title>Hawk-Dove payoff matrix</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v-w}{2},\; \frac{v-w}{2}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(v,\; 0)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0,\; v)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{v}{2},\; \frac{v}{2}\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        In <xref ref="hw-hawk-dove" /> we let <m>v</m> vary. In this
        exercise we assume <m>0 \lt v \lt w</m>, which is the standard
        assumption for the Hawk-Dove game.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-nash-dominated">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Under the assumption <m>0 \lt v \lt w</m>, identify any weakly or
          strictly dominated strategies, or briefly explain why none exist.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Your work on <xref ref="hw-hawk-dove" /> is a good place to start.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-nash-find">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Under the assumption <m>0 \lt v \lt w</m>, find all Nash
          equilibria of the game. State each Nash equilibrium as a strategy
          profile, not just the payoffs it produces.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-hawk-dove-nash-words">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each Nash equilibrium you found in part (b), explain
          informally, in words, why neither animal wants to switch. Write
          one sentence for each animal, in the context of the fight over
          territory: what happens to that animal now, and what would happen
          if it switched on its own while the other animal stayed put.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-three-player-best-response">
    <title>A Three Player Game, Using Best Response</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a three player game in normal form where each player has
        two strategies, so <m>S_i = \{A, B\}</m> for each player <m>i</m>.
        This has the same setup as <xref ref="hw-three-player-iesds" />,
        but the payoffs are different. Payoffs are listed in the order
        (player 1, player 2, player 3).
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-three-player-br-a">
        <title>Player 3 plays <m>A</m></title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>A</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>B</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>A</m></cell>
            <cell>(10, 20, 15)</cell>
            <cell>(5, 20, 10)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>B</m></cell>
            <cell>(20, 15, 10)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 10, 10)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-three-player-br-b">
        <title>Player 3 plays <m>B</m></title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>A</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>B</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>A</m></cell>
            <cell>(20, 20, 20)</cell>
            <cell>(25, 20, 15)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>B</m></cell>
            <cell>(15, 20, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(30, 25, 30)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Use best responses to identify all Nash equilibria. For each Nash
        equilibrium, clearly state the strategy profile and the payoffs for
        that profile.
      </p>

      <p>
        Present your solution by copying the two tables and boxing the best
        response payoffs. Please do not write out each step in words; the boxed
        tables are the clear and concise way to show your work.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-pick-a-number">
    <title>Pick a Number</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A large group of players simultaneously choose an integer from
        <m>\{1, 2, \ldots, 100\}</m>. Let <m>\bar{s}</m> be the average of
        all the choices.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            The player whose choice is nearest to <m>\frac{3}{4}\bar{s}</m>
            gets a payoff of 4. All other players get 0.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If there is a tie, every player who is nearest to
            <m>\frac{3}{4}\bar{s}</m> gets a payoff of 4.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        We make one simplifying assumption: there are so many players that
        a single player's choice has a negligible effect on the average. So
        if one player <m>i</m> switches from <m>s_i</m> to <m>s_i'</m>
        while all other players stay put, the average <m>\bar{s}</m> does
        not change.
      </p>

      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> This assumption cannot be exactly true for a
        finite number of players, but in practice the effect of one player
        is negligible once there are more than about 50 players. With at
        least 150 players, one player's switch cannot move the average
        enough to change who wins (other than possibly moving that one
        player into or out of the winning group).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-pick-a-number-nash">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that for any <m>k \in \{1, \ldots, 100\}</m>, every player
          choosing <m>k</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notation: write <m>\pi_i(s_i, \bar{k})</m> for player <m>i</m>'s
          payoff when player <m>i</m> chooses <m>s_i</m> and every other
          player chooses <m>k</m>. By our assumption, the average is
          <m>\bar{s} = k</m> no matter what <m>s_i</m> is.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-pick-a-number-strict">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For which values of <m>k</m> is the Nash equilibrium from part (a)
          a strict Nash equilibrium? For each such <m>k</m>, clearly show
          that the equilibrium is strict.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Look for a value of <m>k</m> in <m>\{1, 2, \ldots, 100\}</m> for
          which every other choice puts a player farther from
          <m>\frac{3}{4}</m> of the average.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-first-price-auction">
    <title>A First-Price Auction</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In Section 2.7 (<xref ref="read-2-7-part1" />) we showed that in a
        second-price auction, bidding your value weakly dominates every
        other strategy. Here we look at a <term>first-price</term> auction,
        where the winner pays their <em>own</em> bid. The setup:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            There are <m>N \geq 3</m> players. Player <m>i</m> values the
            object at <m>v_i \gt 0</m>, and the values are ranked
            <me>v_1 \geq v_2 \geq v_3 \geq \cdots \geq v_N \gt 0.</me>
            All players know this ranking.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Each player submits one sealed bid <m>b_i \geq 0</m>, without
            seeing anyone else's bid. So <m>S_i = [0, \infty)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The highest bid wins. The winner pays their bid and gets
            payoff <m>v_i - b_i</m>. Everyone else pays nothing and gets
            <m>0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Ties go to the player with the lowest index. For example, if
            players 2 and 4 tie for the highest bid, player 2 wins and
            player 4 gets 0.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Let <m>h_i = \max_{j \neq i} b_j</m> be the highest bid among the
        <em>other</em> players. Then player <m>i</m>'s payoff is
        <me>
          \pi_i(b_i, b_{-i}) =
          \begin{cases}
            v_i - b_i, \amp b_i \gt h_i \\
            v_i - b_i, \amp b_i = h_i \text{ and } i \text{ is the lowest index with bid } h_i \\
            0, \amp \text{otherwise.}
          \end{cases}
        </me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-first-price-auction-nash">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the strategy profile with <m>b_1 = v_2</m> and
          <m>b_i = v_i</m> for all <m>i \geq 2</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-first-price-auction-above">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that any bid <m>b_i \gt v_i</m> is weakly dominated by the
          bid <m>b_i' = v_i</m>. Make sure you show that <em>both</em>
          conditions in the definition of a weakly dominated strategy hold.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-first-price-auction-at">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that the bid <m>b_i = v_i</m> is weakly dominated by any bid
          <m>b_i'</m> with <m>0 \lt b_i' \lt v_i</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

<interactive xml:id="question-form-3-ex" iframe="question-form.html?section=chapter 3 exercises" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</exercises>
